+# ---- pixel background, added for 25_invisible_text -------------------------
+# THE VECTOR PATH CANNOT SEE A GOOGLE EXPORT'S BACKGROUND.
+#
+# Measured 25 Nov 2026 on 34 real Google exports: every page carries exactly ONE filled
+# rect and it is page-sized - the sheet Google paints itself. fill_rects() therefore
+# yields no band, `behind` is None for every run on every page, and the check returns 0.
+# That is why it has not failed a Google export in 392 of them. The bands ARE there;
+# Google rasterises them, so a navy table header that arrives as an image is invisible
+# to get_drawings().
+#
+# So the background is read from the rendered pixels instead. The constants are measured,
+# not guessed - see FINDINGS/INVISIBLE-TEXT-FALSIFIABLE-25NOV.md:
+#
+#   BG_RING_PT  0.5   the sample ring sits this far outside the run's ink box. The radius
+#                     is NOT what separates a real defect from a neighbour: on
+#                     340_bill_of_lading the ring reads 61 % black / 39 % white at 0.5 pt
+#                     AND at 3.5 pt, because that run sits on a barcode. Growing the
+#                     radius only reaches further into the neighbour.
+#   BG_RING_MAX 1.5   never sample further out than this, even for a tiny run.
+#   BG_PURITY   0.90  the background must be at least 90 % one colour. A mixed background
+#                     is not a background, so the check ABSTAINS instead of comparing the
+#                     run against whichever colour wins the vote. This is the rule that
+#                     keeps 340 (barcode, 61 %) out and mutual-fund-statement in (100 %).
+#   BG_TOL      0.08  per channel, unchanged from the vector rule.
+# Same answer for every tolerance 0.04-0.12 and every purity 0.80-0.95, and at 72, 100 and
+# 150 dpi: 2 documents fail, the same 2.
+BG_DPI = 100
+BG_RING_PT = 0.5
+BG_RING_MAX = 1.5
+BG_PURITY = 0.90
+BG_TOL = 0.08
+BG_MIN_PX = 12
+
+
